    "magnitude": "minor",
    "time_horizon": "long-term",
    "confidence": "low",
    "plain": "Banning fracking and new coal mines has very little near-term cost to living standards because fracking was unlikely to lower energy prices anyway and the last coal power station has already closed. The main caveat is the impact on coking coal for steel, with long-term clean-energy job gains remaining a government projection rather than a settled fact.",
    "biggest_unknown": "Whether banning coking coal extraction materially harms UK steel-sector competitiveness and associated jobs, or whether green-transition job creation projections materialise at the scale and in the communities claimed.",
    "claims": [
      {
        "tier": "stated",
        "claim": "The policy maintains the fracking ban and introduces a ban on new coal mines.",
        "evidence_id": "M",
        "quote": "Maintaining the ban on fracking and introducing a ban on new coal mines."
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "There is expert consensus that fracking would not significantly reduce UK energy bills or enhance energy security in the short to medium term.",
